{"version":"6.9","hitCount":1,"request":{"queryString":"EXT_ID:21315951 AND SRC:MED","internalQuery":"EXT_ID:21315951 AND SRC:MED","resultType":"CORE","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":25,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"21315951","source":"MED","pmid":"21315951","pmcid":"PMC6541915","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC6541915"]},"doi":"10.1016/j.jaad.2010.06.045","title":"Radiofrequency facial rejuvenation: evidence-based effect.","authorString":"el-Domyati M, el-Ammawi TS, Medhat W, Moawad O, Brennan D, Mahoney MG, Uitto J.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"el-Domyati M","lastName":"el-Domyati","firstName":"Moetaz","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8921-2442"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Dermatology, Al-Minya University, Al-Minya, Egypt."}]}},{"fullName":"el-Ammawi TS","lastName":"el-Ammawi","firstName":"Tarek S","initials":"TS"},{"fullName":"Medhat W","lastName":"Medhat","firstName":"Walid","initials":"W","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6704-2171"}},{"fullName":"Moawad O","lastName":"Moawad","firstName":"Osama","initials":"O","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-9483-7744"}},{"fullName":"Brennan D","lastName":"Brennan","firstName":"Donna","initials":"D"},{"fullName":"Mahoney MG","lastName":"Mahoney","firstName":"My G","initials":"MG"},{"fullName":"Uitto J","lastName":"Uitto","firstName":"Jouni","initials":"J"}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6704-2171"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8921-2442"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-9483-7744"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","recommended_reviews","wikipedia"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"3","volume":"64","journalIssueId":1807972,"dateOfPublication":"2011 Mar","monthOfPublication":3,"yearOfPublication":2011,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Am Acad Dermatol","title":"Journal of the American Academy of Dermatology","essn":"1097-6787","issn":"0190-9622","isoabbreviation":"J Am Acad Dermatol","nlmid":"7907132"},"printPublicationDate":"2011-03-01"},"pubYear":"2011","pageInfo":"524-535","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Multiple therapies involving ablative and nonablative techniques have been developed for rejuvenation of photodamaged skin. Monopolar radiofrequency (RF) is emerging as a gentler, nonablative skin-tightening device that delivers uniform heat to the dermis at a controlled depth.<h4>Objective</h4>We evaluated the clinical effects and objectively quantified the histologic changes of the nonablative RF device in the treatment of photoaging.<h4>Methods</h4>Six individuals of Fitzpatrick skin type III to IV and Glogau class I to II wrinkles were subjected to 3 months of treatment (6 sessions at 2-week intervals). Standard photographs and skin biopsy specimens were obtained at baseline, and at 3 and 6 months after the start of treatment. We performed quantitative evaluation of total elastin, collagen types I and III, and newly synthesized collagen using computerized histometric and immunohistochemical techniques. Blinded photographs were independently scored for wrinkle improvement.<h4>Results</h4>RF produced noticeable clinical results, with high satisfaction and corresponding facial skin improvement. Compared with the baseline, there was a statistically significant increase in the mean of collagen types I and III, and newly synthesized collagen, while the mean of total elastin was significantly decreased, at the end of treatment and 3 months posttreatment.<h4>Limitations</h4>A limitation of this study is the small number of patients, yet the results show a significant improvement.<h4>Conclusions</h4>Although the results may not be as impressive as those obtained by ablative treatments, RF is a promising treatment option for photoaging with fewer side effects and downtime.","affiliation":"Department of Dermatology, Al-Minya University, Al-Minya, Egypt.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","langua